Ray wrote:
> Two observations:  First, the unique items required to win the game
> are sufficiently special that it is worthwhile for a special section
> of the savefile near the beginning to record exactly where they are.
> 
> As matters stand, if *any* part of the savefile is corrupt on disk, 
> you lose the rest of the savefile - and those unique items if they 
> happen to be stored after it.  And let's face it, they usually are.
>  
> Secondly, the savefiles need a major redesign *anyway* for purposes 
> of both robustness and cross-platform compatibility. 
> 
> There are a bunch of encoding tricks that could be used to make 
> savefiles many times more recoverable.  A file header section 
> giving byte offsets of anything that follows a variable-length 
> section would be a good start.  But going cross-platform with an
> ascii-encoded savefile with unique, unambiguous delimiters between 
> sections instead of writing binary data would be even better.  Of 
> course it would be more robust.  But for another thing it would 
> allow people to continue games on machines other than the one on 
> which they started.  And for another, it would make the Hearse's 
> job ever so much easier.
> 
>                                 Bear


I agree that ASCII encoding is definitely the way to go if any
redesign of the savefile is to be done.  Attempts to make the savefile
more robust by including offsets and backpointers and so on would just
create more places for data to become corrupt or out of sync.  But in
ASCII, it'd be immediately clear where the real data ended and
the corruption began. And likewise where the corruption ends and good
data begins again.  Most of the data could probably be represented
without using binary: objects could use key/value pairs or
s-expressions; levels could be described in the same format the level
compiler uses. (I think the level format can also specify monsters
and objects, but I'm not sure how specific it can be. Might just
be things like "random h", "random armor")  But it'd probably be
doable.

The only downside (aside from the time it would take to implement, of
course) is that savescummers would be able to do things like put an
ascension kit on the dlvl1 if they wanted to.  Some things could be
tested for and cause a death by trickery, like if they have dlvl2
as the deepest level but still have the Amulet, but anything that
would stop a determined cheater would also stop most genuine attempts
at recovery.  I don't believe that that by itself is sufficient
reason not to make the change; people still couldn't cheat on public
servers, and if they cheat on their home systems, it's their own loss.
And cross-platform savefile compatibility would be a big enough win to
justify it even without the reliability gains. But it's something to
consider.


